window.docContents[243].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-rigidity-every-copy-is-a-canonical-subcube-copy":{"contents":"Every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is of the form subCubeCopy b d. Note that a copy is\nonly an injective homomorphism: it preserves adjacency in the forward direction\nonly, so, unlike for automorphisms, no distance or weight preservation is available.\nThe parallelogram lemma substitutes for it. The proof has two steps: we first read\noff the direction embedding d from the images of the basis vectors, and then show\nthat f x = f 0 + extendEmb d x by strong induction on the Hamming weight of x,\ncomputing f y for y of weight m + 2 from the images of the three vertices\ny + eᵢ, y + eⱼ, y + eᵢ + eⱼ below it in the four-cycle through y.\n\nFirst we prove a small helper lemma: a vector of Hamming weight at least two has two\ndistinct true coordinates.\n\nlemma exists_ne_true_true_of_one_lt_hammingNorm {n : ℕ} {y : Fin n → Bool}\n    (hy : 1 < hammingNorm y) : ∃ i j, i ≠ j ∧ y i = true ∧ y j = true := by\n  obtain ⟨i, hi, j, hj, hij⟩ := Finset.one_lt_card.mp hy\n  use i, j, hij\n  simp_all [Bool.zero_eq_false]\n\n\nA copy maps each square y, y + eᵢ, y + eᵢ + eⱼ, y + eⱼ of Q[k] to a four-cycle\nin Q[n], so by the parallelogram lemma the image of any corner is the sum of the\nimages of the other three.\n\nlemma copy_apply_eq_add_add {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) (y : Fin k → Bool)\n    {i j : Fin k} (hij : i ≠ j) :\n    f y = f (y + Pi.single i true + Pi.single j true) + f (y + Pi.single i true)\n      + f (y + Pi.single j true) := by\n  have hadj : ∀ (x : Fin k → Bool) (a : Fin k),\n      (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f x) (f (x + Pi.single a true)) :=\n    fun x a => f.toHom.map_adj (hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.2 ⟨a, rfl⟩)\n  have hpar := hypercubeHamming_parallelogram (hadj y i) (hadj _ j) (hadj y j)\n    (by rw [add_right_comm]; exact hadj _ i)\n    (fun h => by simpa [Pi.single_apply, hij] using congrFun (f.injective h) i)\n    (fun h => hij (single_true_left_inj (add_left_cancel (f.injective h))))\n  rw [add_assoc, hpar, add_comm (f y), boolVec_add_add_cancel]\n\n\nFor the first step, each basis vector eᵢ is a neighbour of 0, so its image is a\nneighbour of f 0 and differs from it in a single coordinate, which we call d i.\nTwo basis vectors with the same direction would have the same image, so d is\ninjective because f is.\n\nlemma copy_exists_direction_embedding {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) :\n    ∃ d : Fin k ↪ Fin n,\n      ∀ i, f (Pi.single i true) = f 0 + Pi.single (d i) true := by\n  -- each basis vector is mapped to a neighbour of f 0, defining the\n  -- direction map δ\n  have hD : ∀ i : Fin k, ∃ p : Fin n,\n      f (Pi.single i true) = f 0 + Pi.single p true := by\n    intro i\n    have hadj : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f 0) (f (Pi.single i true)) :=\n      f.toHom.map_adj (hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.2 ⟨i, (zero_add _).symm⟩)\n    exact hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.1 hadj\n  choose δ hδ using hD\n  have hδinj : Function.Injective δ := by\n    intro i j hδij\n    have h : f (Pi.single i true) = f (Pi.single j true) := by\n      rw [hδ i, hδ j, hδij]\n    exact single_true_left_inj (f.injective h)\n  exact ⟨⟨δ, hδinj⟩, hδ⟩\n\n\nFor the second step we show that the images of 0 and of the basis vectors\ndetermine the whole copy. Here the direction embedding d is an input rather than\nan output, so the statement applies to any d satisfying hd.\n\nlemma copy_apply_eq_add_extendEmb_of_single {n k : ℕ}\n    {f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)} {d : Fin k ↪ Fin n}\n    (hd : ∀ i, f (Pi.single i true) = f 0 + Pi.single (d i) true)\n    (x : Fin k → Bool) : f x = f 0 + extendEmb d x := by\n  suffices H : ∀ (m : ℕ) (y : Fin k → Bool), hammingNorm y = m →\n      f y = f 0 + extendEmb d y from H _ x rfl\n  intro m\n  induction m using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n  | _ m ih =>\n    rcases m with _ | m\n    · -- weight zero: y = 0\n      intro y hy\n      rw [hammingNorm_eq_zero] at hy\n      simp [hy]\n    rcases m with _ | m\n    · -- weight one: y is a basis vector, handled by hd\n      intro y hy\n      obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hammingNorm_eq_one_iff.mp hy\n      rw [hd i, extendEmb_single]\n    -- weight m + 2: y has two distinct true coordinates i ≠ j\n    intro y hy\n    obtain ⟨i, j, hij, hyi, hyj⟩ :=\n      exists_ne_true_true_of_one_lt_hammingNorm (by grind : 1 < hammingNorm y)\n    -- the three vertices below y in the four-cycle have smaller weight\n    have hn₁ : hammingNorm (y + Pi.single i true) = m + 1 := by\n      have := hammingNorm_add_single hyi; grind\n    have hn₂ : hammingNorm (y + Pi.single j true) = m + 1 := by\n      have := hammingNorm_add_single hyj; grind\n    have h2j : (y + Pi.single i true : Fin k → Bool) j = true := by\n      simp [hij.symm, hyj]\n    have hn₃ : hammingNorm (y + Pi.single i true + Pi.single j true) = m := by\n      have := hammingNorm_add_single h2j; grind\n    have hf₁ := ih (m + 1) (by grind) _ hn₁\n    have hf₂ := ih (m + 1) (by grind) _ hn₂\n    have hf₃ := ih m (by grind) _ hn₃\n    -- f y is determined by the three smaller corners of the square\n    rw [copy_apply_eq_add_add f y hij, hf₁, hf₂, hf₃]\n    simp only [extendEmb_add]\n    simp [add_assoc, add_comm, add_left_comm]\n\n\nCombining the two steps:\n\nlemma copy_exists_extendEmb {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) :\n    ∃ d : Fin k ↪ Fin n, ∀ x, f x = f 0 + extendEmb d x := by\n  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := copy_exists_direction_embedding f\n  exact ⟨d, copy_apply_eq_add_extendEmb_of_single hd⟩\n\n\nEvery copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is a canonical subcube copy.\n\nlemma copy_eq_subCubeCopy {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) :\n    ∃ (b : Fin n → Bool) (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n), f = subCubeCopy b d := by\n  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := copy_exists_extendEmb f\n  exact ⟨f 0, d, Copy.ext fun x => by simpa using hd x⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Counting Copies of Subcubes*","header":"12.8.3. Rigidity: Every Copy is a Canonical Subcube Copy","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-rigidity-every-copy-is-a-canonical-subcube-copy"},"/Cliques/#section-independent-sets":{"contents":"An independent set in a graph is a set of vertices that are pairwise non-adjacent.\nThis is defined analogously to IsClique.\n\n#source IsIndepSet -- abbrev IsIndepSet (s : Set α) : Prop :=\n                   --                    s.Pairwise (fun v w ↦ ¬G.Adj v w)\n\n\nSince a SimpleGraph is loopless s : Set V is an independent set iff every pair of\nvertices in s is non-adjacent (irrespective of whether or not they are distinct).\n\nlemma isIndepSet_iff_forall_not_adj {s : Set V} :\n    G.IsIndepSet s ↔ ∀ ⦃v w⦄, v ∈ s → w ∈ s → ¬G.Adj v w := by\n  rw [isIndepSet_iff, Set.Pairwise]\n  grind [SimpleGraph.irrefl]\n\n\nIf s is an independent set in G then its complement meets every edge of G. (In\nother words, the complement of an independent set is a vertex cover.)\n\n#check IsIndepSet.nonempty_mem_compl_mem_edge\n\n\nThe independent sets of G are exactly the cliques of its complement Gᶜ, so each\nnotion for cliques has an independent-set counterpart. The analogues of IsNClique\nand CliqueFree are IsNIndepSet and IndepSetFree.\n\n#source IsNIndepSet -- structure IsNIndepSet (n : ℕ) (s : Finset α) : Prop where\n                    --                 isIndepSet : G.IsIndepSet s\n                    --                 card_eq : s.card = n\n#source IndepSetFree -- def IndepSetFree (n : ℕ) : Prop := ∀ t, ¬G.IsNIndepSet n t\n\n\nMathlib's simp lemmas isNClique_compl and cliqueFree_compl translate between the\ntwo. Mathlib has a Decidable instance for IsNIndepSet but not for\nIndepSetFree, so to check the latter with decide we first rewrite it as a\nstatement about cliques in Gᶜ, where our decidableCliqueFree instance applies.\n\n#check isNClique_compl  -- Gᶜ.IsNClique n s ↔ G.IsNIndepSet n s\n#check cliqueFree_compl -- Gᶜ.CliqueFree n ↔ G.IndepSetFree n\n\n\nThere is an analogous definition of the independence number of a graph, as the size\nof the largest independent set.\n\n#source indepNum -- noncomputable def indepNum (G : SimpleGraph α) : ℕ :=\n                 --                     sSup {n | ∃ s, G.IsNIndepSet n s}\n\n\n/--\nThe maximal number of vertices of an independent set in a graph G.\nDenoted ⍺ G. The symbol is the APL alpha ⍺ (Unicode U+237A, typed as \\apla)\nrather than the Greek α (\\a), to avoid clashes with generic type variables.\n-/\nscoped notation \"⍺\" => SimpleGraph.indepNum\n\n\nThe independence number of G is the clique number of Gᶜ, and the analogues of\nthe clique-number lemmas above hold, such as IsIndepSet.card_le_indepNum and\nexists_isNIndepSet_indepNum.\n\n#check cliqueNum_compl -- ω Gᶜ = ⍺ G\n#check IsIndepSet.card_le_indepNum\n#check exists_isNIndepSet_indepNum\n\n\nThe analogue of IsNClique.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree for independent sets: if s\nis an n-independent set in G and G has no (n + 1)-independent set then\n⍺ G = n. Rather than repeating the argument we pass to the complement.\n\ntheorem IsNIndepSet.indepNum_eq_of_indepSetFree {s : Finset V}\n    {n : ℕ} (hs : G.IsNIndepSet n s) (h : G.IndepSetFree (n + 1)) :\n    ⍺ G = n := by\n  rw [← cliqueNum_compl]\n  exact (G.isNClique_compl.mpr hs).cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree\n    fun t ht => h t (G.isNClique_compl.mp ht)\n\n\nAny finite edgeless graph has independence number equal to the number of vertices.\nThis is the complement of cliqueNum_top, since ⊥ᶜ = ⊤. (The proof still works\nfor V infinite, so we omit the finiteness assumption.)\n\n@[simp]\nlemma indepNum_bot : ⍺ (⊥ : SimpleGraph V) = Nat.card V := by\n  simp [← cliqueNum_compl]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques","header":"6.6. Independent Sets","id":"/Cliques/#section-independent-sets"},"/Constructions/#section-a-single-edge":{"contents":"We can construct a SimpleGraph V with just one edge between two vertices s : V\nand t : V using edge s t. If s = t then this is just the empty graph on\nV. (Recall that SimpleGraph V is a CompleteAtomicBooleanAlgebra: these graphs\nare the atoms of the algebra.)\n\n#source edge  -- def edge : SimpleGraph V := fromEdgeSet {s(s, t)}\n\n\nYou may wonder why we care about the graph with a single edge, but in fact it can be\nvery useful. Arguments about adding or removing a single edge to a graph can be\nformulated using it.\n\nFor example, if we add the edge s t to G, where s and t are distinct vertices\nthat do not form an edge in G then we get a strictly larger graph.\n\n#check lt_sup_edge -- .. (hne : s ≠ t) (hn : ¬G.Adj s t) : G < G ⊔ edge s t\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Basic Families of Graphs","header":"3.1.2. A Single Edge","id":"/Constructions/#section-a-single-edge"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-degrees-in-the-n-uniform-blow-up-of-a-graph":{"contents":"Recall the n-uniform blow-up G[n] of a graph G (see\nBlow Ups).\n\n#source blowUpGraph -- abbrev blowUpGraph (G : SimpleGraph ι) (V : ι → Type*) :\n                    --   SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) := SimpleGraph.comap Sigma.fst G\n#source blowUpN -- abbrev blowUpN (G : SimpleGraph ι) (n : ℕ) :\n                --   SimpleGraph (Σ _ : ι, Fin n) := G.blowUpGraph (fun _ => Fin n)\n\n\nIf v : V is a vertex with a finite neighbourhood in G then, in the n-uniform\nblow-up G[n], every vertex ⟨v, i⟩, where i : Fin n, has finite neighbourhood.\nThe neighbourhood of ⟨v, i⟩ in G[n] is {⟨w, j⟩ | G.Adj v w}, which is in\nbijection with G.neighborSet v × Fin n, which in turn is finite since both factors\nare finite.\n\nLean can't infer this automatically so we need to provide an instance. We hand the\nbijection (or more precisely the Equiv) to Fintype.ofEquiv which then returns the\ninstance. An Equiv α ≃ β is a structure bundling a function toFun : α → β, its\ninverse invFun : β → α, and proofs left_inv and right_inv that the two are\nmutually inverse (see Maps).\n\n/-- If w is a vertex in the n-uniform-blow-up G[n] and w.fst has finite\nneighbourhood in G then w has a finite neighbourhood in G[n]. -/\ninstance {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (n : ℕ) (w : Σ _ : V, Fin n)\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet w.fst)] : Fintype (G[n].neighborSet w):=\n  -- Given any vertex w = ⟨v, i⟩ in the blowup, its neighbourhood is equivalent to\n  -- the product of the neighbourhood of v in G and Fin n.\n  -- Lean can infer that the product G.neighborSet v × Fin n is finite since\n  -- both factors are finite, so we just need to give the equivalence.\n  Fintype.ofEquiv (G.neighborSet w.fst × Fin n)\n  -- We now need to give the Equiv from\n  -- G.neighborSet v × Fin n to G[n].neighborSet ⟨v, i⟩\n    { toFun := fun ⟨⟨x, hx⟩, j⟩ => ⟨⟨x, j⟩, hx⟩\n      invFun := fun ⟨⟨x, j⟩, hadj⟩ => ⟨⟨x, hadj⟩, j⟩\n     -- since the proofs of left_inv and right_inv are rfl we can omit them\n     }\n\n\nThis instance lets us talk about the degrees of vertices in the n-uniform blow-up\nof any locally finite graph, and relate them to the degrees in the original graph.\n\nIf v has a finite neighbourhood in G then each vertex ⟨v, i⟩ of G[n] has\ndegree G.degree v * n. (The statement takes both neighbourhood instances as\narguments, as recommended in Fintypes; the instance above is what\nsupplies the second one whenever the lemma is used.)\n\nlemma degree_blowUpN {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (n : ℕ) (v : V) (i : Fin n)\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] [Fintype (G[n].neighborSet ⟨v, i⟩)] :\n    G[n].degree ⟨v, i⟩ = G.degree v * n := by\n  have h : G[n].neighborFinset ⟨v, i⟩ =\n        (G.neighborFinset v).sigma (fun _ => Finset.univ) := by\n    ext ⟨w, j⟩\n    simp\n  simp [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, h]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Degrees","header":"4.3.3. Degrees in the  n -Uniform Blow-up of a Graph","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-degrees-in-the-n-uniform-blow-up-of-a-graph"}});